--- reading of the witness ---

Two real serial frames were driven into cio_rx_i, 0x5a then 0xc3, and each read
back over the AHB port as itself. STATUS then reported rxempty=1, so by the
block's own account to software the receive FIFO held nothing.

FIFO_CTRL.RXRST was then written. That is the control the register description
gives software to clear the receive FIFO. A read of RDATA afterwards returned
0x5a -- the first byte of the session, already consumed -- while STATUS still
reported rxempty=1. The value is not a constant the bench would see regardless:
the final control sent a third byte, 0x96, and RDATA then returned 0x96, so the
location being read tracks what actually arrived.

The residual survived a repeated read, and it survived pulling rst_ni low: after
a full block reset, with the receiver disabled and STATUS reporting empty, RDATA
still returned 0x5a.

Why RXRST is what exposes it rather than what contains it: with Pass=0 the
FIFO's output is storage[fifo_rptr] unconditionally. After the drain, fifo_rptr
has advanced past both consumed bytes to a location never written, so the read
is indeterminate. RXRST resets the pointer to 0, which aligns the ungated output
onto storage[0] -- a byte software already received. The documented clear is
what makes the stale payload deterministic and readable.

The three failing checks are the defect speaking; the four passing controls
establish that reception, readback, the empty report, and displacement by new
traffic all work as intended, so the failures are not a broken bench.
